---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="10-decomposition"
  lessonSlug="02-area-shapes"
  hook="Three one-line helpers + one orchestrator. The orchestrator dispatches by kind and CALLS the helpers — no inline math."
>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`import math

def area_rectangle(w, h):  return w * h
def area_triangle(b, h):   return 0.5 * b * h
def area_circle(r):        return math.pi * r ** 2

def total_area(shapes):
    total = 0.0
    for shape in shapes:
        kind = shape[0]
        if kind == "rect":
            total += area_rectangle(shape[1], shape[2])
        elif kind == "tri":
            total += area_triangle(shape[1], shape[2])
        elif kind == "circ":
            total += area_circle(shape[1])
    return total

shapes = [("rect", 2, 3), ("tri", 4, 5), ("circ", 1)]
print(f"Total area: {total_area(shapes):.4f}")     # 19.1416`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="The 'inline the math' anti-pattern">
    <p>
      If you write <code>total += shape[1] * shape[2]</code> inside
      <code>total_area</code> instead of calling <code>area_rectangle</code>,
      you've duplicated the formula. Change the formula → forget the other
      copy → bug. The autograder enforces calling at least 2 helpers.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
